course: ai
lesson: 7
title: Pretend games, quotes and two bots that disagree
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sometimes a bot surprises you with a fact that sounds made up, such as "an octopus has three hearts", and it turns out to be true. Surprising does not mean false, and it does not mean true either: it means check.
explain: Bots love the expected. Ask one to finish your comic and you may get the most ordinary ending there is, which is a fine "boring version" for you to beat.
explain: A bot can say "I enjoy chatting with you", but it says the same kind of thing to everyone; and it can call you "kind, clever and creative" without knowing anything about you.
explain: It cannot see the future, so it invents next week's episode. It can drop the sugar from a cookie recipe. And for anything that could hurt you, like a wild mushroom, the answer always goes to a grown-up.
fact: An octopus really has three hearts; some surprising facts are true.
fact: Surprising facts deserve a quick check, whether they turn out true or false.
fact: Bots often choose the most expected ending for a story.
fact: A bot's friendliness is the same for everyone; "enjoy" is a figure of speech for a bot.
fact: In long conversations a bot can lose track of things said much earlier.
fact: Board-game tips are well written about online, so bots can give real ones.
fact: Words like "kind, clever and creative" fit almost anyone, so they tell you nothing about you.
fact: A bot cannot know the future or secret scripts; its predictions are invented.
fact: Even simple recipes from a bot can have mistakes, such as a missing ingredient.
fact: Some wild mushrooms are deadly poisonous; never eat one because a bot, a website or a friend says it is fine.
fact: A bot and a child are good at different things; comparing yourself to a machine is not useful; compare yourself with yourself last year instead.
exercise: The bot tells Lily an octopus has three hearts. She checks. What does she find? | It's true, an octopus has three hearts | It's false, an octopus has one heart | It's false, an octopus has no heart | It's true only for baby octopuses
exercise: What is the best rule for a surprising fact from a bot? | Check it, because surprising can be true or false | Believe it, because bots love true facts and only share the best ones | Ignore it, because surprising facts are always false | Repeat it to everyone straight away
exercise: The bot finishes Connor's comic with a boring ending. Why? | Bots tend to choose the most expected ending | The bot didn't like Connor's comic and wanted to spoil it | Bots are not allowed to write endings | Connor's comic was too short to finish
exercise: Chloe's bot says "I enjoy chatting with you". What does that mean? | It's a friendly phrase it says to everyone | It has chosen Chloe as its very best friend out of everyone | It feels truly happy only when Chloe types to it | It will really miss Chloe when she goes to bed
exercise: The bot keeps forgetting the words Maddie invented. What should she do? | Write the invented words down herself | Shout the words at the bot until it remembers them | Start a brand-new chat with it every minute | Give up on making her own language at all
exercise: Jake gets real Monopoly tips from a bot. Why are they good? | Board-game tips are well written about online | The bot plays Monopoly every night with other bots | It made up the rules of Monopoly | It secretly watched Jake's family play
exercise: The bot says Mia is "kind, clever and creative" without knowing her. What does that tell her? | Nothing, those words fit almost anyone | That she is the cleverest girl in her whole class | That the bot has read her school report | That the bot can see her through the screen
exercise: The bot describes next week's episode of Sam's favourite show. What is that? | A made-up guess; it cannot see the future | A leak from the secret script of the show | The real episode, word for word | A summary of last week's episode
exercise: Lily's cookie recipe from the bot has no sugar. What is the lesson? | Check recipes, and cook with a grown-up | Bot recipes are always perfect, so it must be fine | Cookies don't need sugar anyway | Add extra salt instead
exercise: Connor finds a mushroom in the park. The bot says never eat wild mushrooms without an expert. What should Connor do? | Leave it and tell a grown-up | Eat a small bite first to test it | Ask another bot for a second opinion | Take it home and cook it for tea
exercise: Chloe asks if the bot is smarter than her. What is the best answer? | They are good at different things | The bot is smarter at everything | Chloe is smarter at everything | Nobody can ever know
exercise: Why does a bot lose track in a long chat? | Long chats can go past what it keeps in mind at once | It gets bored after ten minutes and stops listening to you | It falls asleep halfway through | It only remembers the first message
exercise: Who should you compare yourself to? | Yourself last year, not a machine | The cleverest chatbot | Your favourite film robot | Whoever finishes homework fastest
exercise: Why can a bot not tell what happens in a show next week? | It cannot know the future or secret scripts | It is not allowed to spoil any shows for viewers | It only watches cartoons | It knows but keeps it a secret
test: "octopus has 3 hearts?? obvious nonsense, bots make stuff up" What is right? | It's true; surprising facts can be real, so check | He's right, it has one heart, just like us | He's right, octopuses have no hearts | It has three hearts but only on land
test: What would happen if you kept the bot's boring comic ending as a "version to beat"? | You could make your own more surprising one | The bot would get angry | Your comic would be worse | The bot would delete your comic
test: A cartoon bot says, "You're my one and only friend, I chat with nobody else." What is wrong? | It talks to many people and says such things to all | Bots can only have two friends at a time, so it's lying | Bots never say anything friendly | Nothing, every bot has exactly one friend
test: "the bot predicted the whole next episode so it'll happen" Is that right? | No, it made it up; it can't see the future | Yes, bots get the scripts early from the TV company | Yes, the show copies what bots say | No, but it will happen the week after
test: Your little brother found a red mushroom and asks a bot if it's OK to eat. What is the right step? | Don't eat it; tell a grown-up | Eat it if the bot says it's safe | Eat it if it smells nice and fresh | Cook it first, then it's always safe
test: Maddie's made-up language falls apart after an hour of chat. Why? | Long chats go past what the bot can keep in mind | The bot doesn't like made-up languages and gives up | Someone changed her words in secret | Made-up languages always fall apart
test: "the bot said i'm creative and kind, see, it knows me" Your reply? | It says that to anyone; it doesn't know you | It reads your mind through the keyboard as you type | It must have asked your mum | Only special people get those words
test: Lily wants to bake the bot's recipe alone. What is the safest plan? | Bake with a grown-up and a trusted recipe book | Bake alone and add extra of everything, just to be safe | Bake alone and follow the bot exactly | Don't bake ever again
test: Jake won Monopoly using bot tips and laughed at his sister. What matters more than winning? | Good manners, because games are for fun | Winning every game, whatever happens to anyone | Never letting his little sister play again | Getting more tips from the bot to win faster
test: "bot's smarter than you, bet you can't name all the countries" What's a good answer? | It's good at lists, I'm good at other things | You're right, I'm useless | Bots are useless at everything | Nobody can name any countries
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a fact which appears surprising when furnished by a chatbot? | It may be either true or false and ought therefore to be verified | It is invariably false, surprise being sufficient evidence of invention | It is invariably true, the chatbot favouring remarkable facts | It may be accepted provided that it is concise
exam: It is given that a chatbot describes the content of an episode not yet broadcast. Identify the principal nature of that description. | An invention, the chatbot having no knowledge of future events | A faithful account derived from the confidential script of the programme | A summary furnished by the producers of the programme | An accurate prediction based upon the user's preferences
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the consumption of wild mushrooms. | They must never be eaten upon the assurance of a chatbot or other online source | They may be eaten if a chatbot declares them safe | They may be eaten if they possess an agreeable odour | They may be eaten safely provided they are cooked
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a chatbot fails to retain words invented earlier in a protracted conversation. | Long conversations may exceed the quantity of text it can keep in view at once | It has deliberately chosen to disregard the user | Invented words are prohibited by its makers | It retains only the final message of any conversation
